-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-
 Proper Usage of GetUser & GetScratch commands in CNet Amiga! (3.05c/4.26b+)
-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

    Before I explain how to use the GETUSERS as such, I'd like to remind you
all that these values(both "short" and "long") can also be used with the MCI
{V } command, whereby you specify a GETUSER value right after the "V", as in
these examples:
                    {v1} ---------> Print User's Handle.
                    {v15} --------> The User's Access Group.
                    {v1200016} ---> The User's Age In Years.
                    {v1100468} ---> Number of ROWS on User's Screen.
                    {v2400960} ---> Total Uploaded KiloBytes to the System.
                    ^
                Control-Q

    Additionally, these values can be used with the {L }, {M }, and {T } MCI
commands to LOAD, perform MATH, or TEST the contents of any getuser location.

    Using these "short" GETUSER values, you can create files that can pull
various pieces of information on the CURRENT user, however, if you wish to
use these values to obtain information on OTHER users, you will have to use
the GETSCRATCH command.

    NOTE, however, that not ALL of these values can be used with GETSCRATCH.
Only those marked with an "s" in the first column will return valid data. you
will have to use the LONG, 7-Digit values to obtain any info NOT listed above
as being flagged by an "s", or you will have to use the ADDRESS CNETREXX port
command to extrapulate the info(using GETUSER), while the user is online. More
info on these areas can be found in the other text files.

    Before you can use the GETSCRATCH command, you have to load the user in
question's information into a temporary buffer called the SCRATCH BUFFER.
You do this by using the LOADSCRATCH command, which takes an ID Number for
it's sole NEEDED argument, like this:

LOADSCRATCH 5

    The above command will load all pertinent information pertaining to the
user using ID Number 5 on your system. You need to be aware that when you use
the LOADSCRATCH command, you are putting a LOCK on the Account ID. What this
means is that NO OTHER FILE, and NO OTHER USER may use this accounts data,
INCLUDING the user which HOLDS that ID. If the user with this ID happens to
be ONLINE when your program LOCKS the account, that user's port will seem to
freeze up, until your program has finished doing what it needs to do. (This
same kind of LOCK occurs when you use the EA command to edit a user's account
while they are online as well).

    After the account has been LOCKED, you can then use GETSCRATCH to obtain
any values you wish, using either the "short" or the "long" values listed in 
the included lists.

    It is recommended that you use a SHORT getuser value whenever possible,
because these commands are defined INTERNALLY by CNet itself, so will almost
ALWAYS point to the correct data, while the Long, 7-digit values COULD change
over time as Ray makes more and more modifications to the internal structures.

    When you are FINISHED grabbing the info needed, you MUST remember to 
UNLOCK the account using the SAVESCRATCH command. This will insure that any 
other program that needs to get into that account CAN, and will also allow 
that user to continue on with his/her business(if they were ONLINE), or that 
they will be ABLE to LOGON the next time they DO call.

    By FORGETTING to UNLOCK an account, you are releasing a veritable bomb
into your system, because if either another program or THAT user tries to
access the account, the port that attempted the access will be LOCKED UP!
The only way to recover the port is through a complete System Reset, or by
running a script similar to the following:

                /* Unlock ALL User Accounts */
                  options results
                  getuser 2400088 ; accounts=result
                  do i=1 to accounts
                    savescratch (-i)
                  end i
                /* End */

    The SAVESCRATCH command also takes a sole NEEDED argument, which should
preferably be the SAME ID Number you used in the LOADSCRATCH command. If all
you did was to READ data out of the account, then you can use a NEGATIVE ID
Number value to simply REMOVE the LOCK on the account, like this:

SAVESCRATCH (-5)

    If, however, you made CHANGES to the data and wish these CHANGES to be
SAVED to the account BEFORE it is UNLOCKED, then supply a POSITIVE value:

SAVESCRATCH 5

    Note that when NEGATIVE values are used, they should be given inside of
parenthesis, else the UNLOCK'ing may NOT be successfull.

    If your file will be executed by the CURRENTLY ONLINE user, then use the 
PUTUSER command, in conjunction with the SETOBJECT command when you wish to 
make CHANGES to their data, like this:

SETOBJECT "His unlisted phone number is: 823-9892"

PUTUSER 9

    The above example places the text shown in the "SysOp Comment" for the
current user online. 

    If, however, your file will be run via AutoMaintenance, or by some OTHER
user manually, you'd need to use the Scratch Buffer. Using the PUTSCRATCH and
SETOBJECT commands, like this:

LOADSCRATCH 5

SETOBJECT "His unlisted phone number is: 823-9892"

PUTSCRATCH 9

SAVESCRATCH 5

    Notice the use of PUTSCRATCH, instead of PUTUSER. Remember that when you
are using the SCRATCH BUFFER, all pertinent commands should END in "SCRATCH"

        LOADSCRATCH, GETSCRATCH, PUTSCRATCH, and SAVESCRATCH

    The SETOBJECT command is the only command that is used for BOTH fields
of operation: Current User versus Scratch User.

    Lastly, what happens if you specify a DIFFERENT ID Number for SAVESCRATCH
than you used in LOADSCRATCH?  Well, you'd be making a DUPLICATE copy of the
SCRATCH BUFFER into the NEW ID Number. What?!? Look at this example:

LOADSCRATCH 1
SAVESCRATCH (-1)
SAVESCRATCH 2
SAVESCRATCH 3

    If the above program was run, your first three Accounts would all hold
IDENTICAL information to that shown in ID # 1's account. This is ONE way to
EASILY make a NEW account. It saves having to sign on as a NEW user, when
all you'd THEN have to do would be to EA the new account and change certain
key pieces of info, notably: Serial ID Number, Handle, Real Name, and perhaps
the Password.

    When writing scripts to automatically update pieces of info in numerous
accounts at once, try using the following technique:

/**/;options results
getuser 2400088 ; accounts=result
do i=1 to accounts
  loadscratch i ; status=result
  getscratch 1  ; handle=result
  if status=0 | handle="!" then do
    savescratch (-i) ; iterate i ; end

  /*                                  */
  /*  Enter your work commands here.  */
  /*                                  */

  savescratch i
end i
/* END */

    Using a DO-Loop allows you to process through ALL your user accounts. The
2400088 value tells the script HOW MANY accounts you have to process. The
"status" and "handle" variables allow you to perform a "pre-check" on the
given account to make sure it's a valid account. "status" returns a "1" if
the information was successfully loaded, or else a "0" if there was a problem
loading the data. One possible problem might be if ANOTHER file was currently
working on that account(ie, the account was locked already). If the "handle"
variable contains a "!" (exclamation point), this means that that account has
been KILLED (or DELETED) from the system. (Use my ReVive utility to attempt
to reincarnate that account). If either "status=0", or "handle='!'", then the
loop will SKIP OVER that account, but will UNLOCK the account using the "-i"
value first. If "status=1" and "handle" equals something OTHER than "!", then
the script continues to the "work" portion, which is supplied by you. After 
your portion, the "savescratch i" command is used to both SAVE and UNLOCK the
account, before proceeding to the next account.

    Note that when the account is SAVED using SAVESCRATCH <id>, the ENTIRE
account is saved back to disk and NOT just the info you changed. Being as the
LOADSCRATCH command reads the userdata directly from disk, if a user signs on
to the board, then makes a change to one or more of their personal infos, if
a script is run that uses the SCRATCH commands, if the account is SAVED, it
is possible that the changes made by the user when they logged on may NOT be
saved to disk(as if the user never changed the info).

    Your "work" portion would contain commands like GETSCRATCH, IF-THEN's,
SETOBJECT's, and PUTSCRATCH commands. For instance, the following "work"
portion could be used to copy ALL user's REAL NAMES into their HANDLE fields,
in the event you ran a BBS that did NOT allow for the usage of aliases, or
handles(Note that the following would have to be INSERTED into the above
listed framework BEFORE being executed, else errors will occur):

/* Work STARTS Here */
  getscratch 1 ; handle=result
  getscratch 3 ; realname=result
  if handle=realname then do
    savescratch (-i) ; iterate i ; end
  setobject realname
  putscratch 1
/* Work ENDS Here */

    The above uses GETSCRATCH to obtain both the real name and handle of the
user account being processed. If the two contain identical information, then
the account is UNLOCKED, and the loop proceeds to the next account...

    If, however, the two differ, then the SETOBJECT and PUTSCRATCH commands
are used to copy the REAL NAME into the HANDLE buffer. The SAVESCRATCH that
is used in the "framework" coding above then SAVES and UNLOCKS that account
before proceeding to the next account.

    As one last example, the following coding could be used to DISABLE the
"SuperUser" flag in any account where the Access Group was LESS than 30, while
at the same time ENABLING the "SuperUser" flag if the Access Group EQUALS 30
or 31. (The "SuperUser" flag will be used to control who has access to certain
SysOp functions pertaining to upcoming Internet enhancements made by Ray):

/* Work STARTS Here */
  getscratch 15 ; access=result
  getscratch 1401380 ; abit1=reverse(d2c(result,4))
  superuser=bittst(abit1,0)
  if (access<30 & superuser=0) | (access>29 & superuser=1) then do
    savescratch (-i) ; iterate i ; end
  if access<30 then abit1=bitclr(abit1,0)
    else abit1=bitset(abit1,0)
  setobject c2d(reverse(abit1),4)
  putscratch 1401380
/* Work ENDS Here */

    Here, we grab the users Access group, then pull the second priviledge
block and extrapolate the "SuperUser" flag, which will equal a 0 if they
do NOT have the flag set, or a 1 if they DO have it set.

    If their access is LOW and the flag is NOT set, or if their access is
HIGH and they DO have the flag set, then no work is needed, so we UNLOCK the
account and continue through the loop.

    If changes are needed, we use the BitSET() function to ENABLE the flag if
the user's Access is HIGHER than 29, else we use the BitCLR() function to
DISABLE the flag if their Access is LOWER than 30. We finish the operation by
reconstructing the priviledge block, then write it into memory by using the
PUTSCRATCH command. The SAVESCRATCH given in the "framework" coding above is
used to SAVE the info to disk as well as UNLOCKING the account before it
continues to the next account ID Number.

    Well, that concludes the basic usage of the GetUsers and Scratch Buffer
of CNet Amiga. The remainder of this document is intended for SEASONED ARexx
enthusiasts ONLY, and is NOT meant for amateurs. Please HEED this warning,
else you could SERIOUSLY damage the system structures used on your bbs.

