    Getuser values given in the examples below pertain to BOTH v3.05c AND
v4.26b+ of CNet Amiga, unless specifically stated otherwise...

    The value returned using the x6xxxxx getuser value is of no use to us if
we're dealing with a STRUCTURE. We must read the value in as a x4xxxxx value
instead. As an example, let's take a look at the SELECT BUFFER structure,
which has a getuser value of 1601978. Here's a snip of what that structure
looks like, as shown in the v4.26b "subs.h" file, as we'll need this info
when we wish to work with any of the data WITHIN that structure:

struct SelectType {         /* Select list */
    long    Size;
    char    Title   [32];
    char    Location[96];   /* physical path to file */
    short   Base;           /* physical subboard # */
    UBYTE   SDownFiles;     /* Which accounting schedule ? */
    UBYTE   SDownBytes;
    UBYTE   temp_rem;       /* DS from DS list!! */
    UBYTE   CDROM;          /* Copy to CDROMpath before download? */
    BYTE    FreeStuff;
    UBYTE   AutoKill;       /* 1==mark for amaint deletion when downloaded
                               2==Yank, 3==DELETE when unselected */
    long    ByteDownload;   /* credits to subtract when this */
    short   FileDownload;   /* item is downloaded ... */
                            /* makes * faster by being here */
    struct  IsDate ADate;   /* utility ... AO adopt dates? */
    ULONG   Number;         /* item number */
};

    The first thing we need to do is "map out" the structure, to find the
LENGTH of each data item WITHIN this structure. Below is a table listing
this info:

            Name            Start   End     Type    Length
           ------------------------------------------------
            Size            1       4       long    4
            Title           5       36      char    32
            Location        37      132     char    96
            Base            133     134     short   2
            SDownFiles      135     135     UBYTE   1
            SDownBytes      136     136     UBYTE   1
            temp_rem        137     137     UBYTE   1
            CDROM           138     138     UBYTE   1
            FreeStuff       139     139     BYTE    1
            AutoKill        140     140     UBYTE   1
            ByteDownload    141     144     long    4
            FileDownload    145     146     short   2
            ADate           147     152     struct  6
            Number          153     156     long    4
           ------------------------------------------------

    We use the info given previously in the Intro file to find the LENGTH of
each data item. Note the "ADate" is a structure in itself, but it's a DATE 
format structure, and from the Intro, we know ALL dates are 6 bytes in length, 
which makes it even easier.

    As a check to make sure we've constructed the table correctly, you can
ADD all the values in the "Length" column. This value should MATCH the last
value in the "End" column. This value determines the OVERALL LENGTH of the
entire structure, which happens to be 156 bytes in this example.

    After these steps have been completed, we're ready to start working with
this information, and can now start the coding necessary. The first thing we
need to do is change the 1601978 value into a x4xxxxx value: 1401978. That's
the easy part. We then read in this value as a getuser, as shown below:

         pb=null() ; getuser 1401978 ; start=offset(pb,result)

    The "pb" variable is a starting point in memory we can use. It contains 
the "null" address, which is "$00000000". Using the returned result of our 
getuser, we can then use the OFFSET() function(which is located in the
"rexxsupport.library") to create a "start" variable. This "start" variable 
contains the starting memory location of the Download Select Buffer, in 
character format. If we wanted to work with the second item in the select 
list, we would add 156 to "result", so the line looked like this:

         pb=null() ; getuser 1401978 ; start=offset(pb,result+156)

    We could use a DO-Loop and work with each file in sequence, until we
had completed whatever action we began. (Another getuser value, 1209644,
tells us how many items are IN the select buffer).

    We can now read in the entire 156 bytes into ONE variable, and then use
the PARSE command to get the info we need:

     struct=import(start,156)

     parse var struct size 5 title 37 location 133 base 135 sdownfiles 136,
                      sdownbytes 137 temp 138 cdrom 139 freestuff 140,
                      autokill 141 bytedownload 145 filedownload 147,
                      adate 153 number

    In the above PARSE statement, the commas at the ENDS of each line are
CONTINUATION markers, meaning what comes on the NEXT line is actually a
CONTINUATION of the above line. Note the numbers that FOLLOW each variable
name are the START values for the FOLLOWING variable, and are taken right
out of that table we made above, so "title" starts at the 5th byte, while
"location" starts at the 37th byte, etc.

    Using the PARSE command is not the ONLY step involved. Each TEXT variable
will ALSO have to be STRIP()'ed of any excess "00"x's that may have been used
to pad out that data to the proper lengths. You will also need to use the
C2D() function to CONVERT the CHARACTER values into DECIMAL values if the
data is a NUMERIC value. Here's how the first six variables would look coded
to be as "clean and neat" as possible:

                      size=c2d(size,4)

                     title=strip(title,,"00"x)

                  location=strip(location,,"00"x)

                      base=c2d(base,2)

                sdownfiles=c2d(sdownfiles)

                sdownbytes=c2d(sdownbytes)

    Again, we use the table we made above to find the lengths of the NUMERIC
data. When a numeric value is NOT given in the C2D() function, the default
value of "1" will be used.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    The following is given for EXPERT users, and makes your work a bit faster
as well as taking up less code:

     pb=null() ; getuser 1401978 ; struct=import(offset(pb,result),156)

     parse var struct size 5 title"00"x 36 location"00"x 131 base 133,
                      sdownfiles 134 sdownbytes 135 temp 136 cdrom 137,
                      freestuff 138 autokill 139 bytedownload 143,
                      filedownload 145 adate 151 number

    Note the addition of the "00"x to both the "title" and "location" variable
names. This way, we can do an automatic STRIP() NOW, without having to worry
about it later. What makes this technique a bit more complicated is the fact
that the NUMERIC values we use will DIFFER from those shown in the table we
have been using. Note the "5" is the same, but we're using "36", instead of
"37". This is because we are reading one "00"x, in ADDITION to the "title".
Note that the "131" is two less than the stated value of "133". This is
because the "00"x that we have following "location" makes the SECOND "00"x
that is being read by the PARSE command, so we need to SUBTRACT TWO from
every following value.

    Simply put(if it CAN be simply put), the NUMERIC START VALUE will be
the STATED START VALUE(from our table above), minus the number of "00"x's
that PRECEED the variable name in the PARSE command. (We use 36, because 1
"00"x appears BEFORE the "title", so 37 - 1 = 36. We use 131, because there
are 2 "00"x's that appear before the "base" name, thus 133 - 2 = 131, etc.)

    Again, the above is meant for EXPERT users, so don't be dismayed if you
happen to be thoroughly LOST about now. Simply use the first method that was
discussed above, followed by the individual STRIP()'ing and/or C2D()'ing
that may be necessary.

