    Finally, let's take a look at some SPECIFIC values in the GetUser list:

======================================================================
   v3.05c   |   The START of the MainPort STRUCTURE      |  v4.26b+
============|============================================|============
   1601850  | Pointer to cnp, MainPort                   |  1601850
------------+--------------------------------------------+------------
            |    The START of the PortData STRUCTURE     |
------------+--------------------------------------------+------------
   2624286  | PortData; (*PortZ[100]) Struct - Port 0    |  2626974
        +4  |                                            |       +4
        |   | (Pointer in MEMORY where struct is stored) |       |
        +4  |                                            |       +4
   2624682  | PortData; (*PortZ[100]) Struct - Port 99   |  2627370
======================================================================

    These values can give us the needed START MEMORY addresses for ALL the
getuser Values given in the "GU40_Longs" listing. The "MainPort" value can
be used to grab any value shown as a 2xxxxxx getuser, while the "PortData"
values govern all the 1xxxxxx getuser values.

    The CNet Manual talks about using the "ADDRESS CNETREXXport" command to
communicate with other CNet ports, either from another CNet Port, or from
an external ARexx script. This command has it's uses, but also has some BIG
drawbacks. If YOUR ARexx file tries to address another port that is ALREADY
RUNNING an ARexx file, then YOUR application is PUT ON HOLD, until THEIR
program has exited. What this means is YOUR port would be LOCKED OUT, until
such a time as your file could get CONTROL of THEIR port. Another problem
comes when your file tries to address a port that is in the middle of a file
transfer(upload or download), as CNet places a LOCK on a port before it will
initiate any file transfer. AGAIN, you'd be locked out, until the completion
of the file transfer.

    If all you WANT to do is READ a certain value from THEIR config data, the
POINTERS above are what you really want to use. Let's say you're wondering if
Port # 1 is HIDING from you(on Port # 0). [This DIFFERS from the WHO display,
which only shows you which ports YOU are hiding from!] Here's all we have to
do to find out this information:

1: Hiding capability is part of the "PortData" structure, which means we
   need to get the START value of Port # 1's "PortData", which the above
   getuser list states is: 2626978(v4.26b), or 2624290(v3.05).

2: We then need to find the HIDING getuser value, which by looking at the
   getuser list, we find is: 1101630 (For Port # 0, because we want to know
   if this port is hiding FROM port 0).

3: To find the OFFSET value, simply DROP the first two numbers from this
   value, along with any preceeding zeroes, and we get: 1630

4: We then convert the x6xxxxx in step 1 to an x4xxxxx, and use THIS value
   in a getuser:

        pb=null() ; getuser 2426978 /* for CNet, v4.26b, or */
        pb=null() ; getuser 2424290 /* for CNet, v3.05c BBS */

        a=import(offset(pb,result+1630),1) ; hide=BitTST(a,0)

5: The "hide" variable would equal a "0" if the user on Port # 1 was NOT
   hiding from you, or a "1" if they WERE hiding from you.

6: The beauty of this whole process is that it's IMMEDIATE, regardless of
   what Port # 1 happens to be doing when you "spy" on them! hehe

