    Lastly, it is also possible to WRITE information directly to MEMORY by
using the STORAGE() command. In fact, every time you use the EA, EG, EL, etc.
commands to make changes to these areas, the changes are being made to the
data in MEMORY, and aren't written to disk until AFTER the user has logged
off. Be VERY, VERY careful when using the STORAGE() command, because using a
wrong value within this command could seriously damage your data files, or
even guru your machine. The STORAGE() command is NOT meant for the beginner.
Anyone can play around with the IMPORT() command. You won't hurt anything by
simply READING the MEMORY, but only WRITE to MEMORY if you KNOW WHAT YOU ARE
DOING! OK, with that "warning" out of the way, here's an example of using
the STORAGE() command:

           Time Left This Call (Mins / by 10) ===> getuser 1200028

    Using the above getuser value, here's a routine that could be run from
any port to automatically give the user on Port # 2 ten extra minutes of
OnLine time: 

            1:  getuser 2426982  /* Use 2424294 on CNet, v3.05c */
            2:  off=offset(null(),result+28)
            3:  data=import(off,2)
            4:  time=c2d(data)%10
            5:  time=time+10
            6:  time=d2c(time*10,2)
            7:  data=storage(off,time,2)

            1:  getuser 2426982  /* Use 2424294 on CNet, v3.05c */
             :
             :  Get START of "PortData" structure.

            2:  off=offset(null(),result+28)
             :
             :  Create the proper OffSet Memory Location, per the given
             :  1200028 getuser value.

            3:  data=import(off,2)
             :
             :  Read in the 2 bytes stored at this location. (Remember, we
             :  have 2 bytes, because it's an x2xxxxx getuser value!)

            4:  time=c2d(data)%10
             :
             :  Convert this returned character to DECIMAL, then divide by
             :  10 to get MINUTES left online.

            5:  time=time+10
             :
             :  Now ADD the extra 10 minutes to current value.

            6:  time=d2c(time*10,2)
             :
             :  Convert DECIMAL back into CHARACTER, but AFTER we multiply
             :  by 10 to go back to TENTHS of MINUTES left online.

            7:  data=storage(off,time,2)
             :
             :  Write the 2 bytes back to MEMORY. Note the likeness between
             :  this command, which WRITES the data, and the import() we
             :  used in line 3 above. Only difference is the inclusion of
             :  the "time" variable, as THIS is the data to be WRITTEN to
             :  memory.

    The important thing to remember when using STORAGE() is to do things in
the ABSOLUTE REVERSE of the way you IMPORT()'ed them. If you use C2D() to get
a DECIMAL value to work with, make sure it goes BACK to a D2C(), etc. Using
STORAGE(), instead of EXPORT() saves us from having to do TWO major jobs:
having to repad text strings with "00"x's, and having to use the cryptic
x2c() and d2x() commands to make the needed character value. Both are now
unnecessary steps to the process! It can get a bit tricky, but if you TAKE
YOUR TIME, and play around with the IMPORT() function FIRST, you can then
MAKE SURE you are using the correct method to GET the value, BEFORE you try
coding the STORAGE() which will then PUT the data(Remember, you won't hurt
ANYTHING if you use IMPORT(), but you could do SEVERE damage with STORAGE(),
unless you have a FIRM GRASP on these principles!)

